      "intro": {
        "lead": [
          "Between pretending to be alive and pretending to be dead,",
          "poems that brush the ink off the hand and walk on"
        ],
        "text": "Dongwon Nam’s poems sort what breathes from what merely moves. Among building walls where something brittle crumbles off at a touch, trash pushed along by the wind and wheels rolling at a steady speed, the poet asks whether to pretend to be alive or to pretend to be dead — and, instead of answering, brushes the ink off his hand. Firm sentences that set down what he has seen, without putting feeling first, are the foundation of this collection."
      },
